  "worked_solution": "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P),\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R,$ where the lower and upper sums respectively are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}\u0394{x}_{i}\u0394{y}_{j} \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}\u0394{x}_{i}\u0394{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe need to compute $U(f,P).$ \n\nNotice that for all subregions ${R}_{ij},$ we have that\n\n- $f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{M}_{ij}=f({x}_{i},{y}_{j})=3{x}_{i}^{2}+{y}_{j}^{2}\n$$\n\nand the upper Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(3{x}_{i}^{2}+{y}_{j}^{2})\u0394{x}_{i}\u0394{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $3{x}_{i}^{2}+{y}_{j}^{2}.$",
